CORE
typedef_uses.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ derived-to-base conversion: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ initializer_list element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.inst]/2 (Note 2): a conversion between pointers to class
types depends on the inheritance relationship, and list-initialization
of a class depends on its constructors, so both instantiate the class
template specialization when only a member typedef has named it so far
(the shapes of libstdc++'s _Rb_tree::_Link_type and map::value_type,
cpp11_set_iterate and cpp11_initializer_list_of_class_elements_argument).
standard_conversion_pointer and brace_init_is_viable now instantiate it.
bin53 rejects the program.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program,
and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
